var e=`# CHAPTER 7 — The Fear

— I tried to buy your love.

He said it just like that, no dressing it up.

— Without Elizabeth next to me to hold me back, I turned into a dumb father. I was terrified you wouldn't like me. Terrified. So I convinced myself that if I gave you everything, you'd love me. Every present was an apology for not knowing how to do the rest.

— And it worked for a while. You loved me and you loved the stuff along with me, and I couldn't tell the two apart anymore.

---

— When you were nine I realized how big a fuck-up I'd made.

He used the hard word only once, and that was it.

— You'd become an impossible child. Not out of meanness — you were a kid. But you were abusing a power that wasn't yours. A status that wasn't yours. Everything you had, Nina, I was the one who earned it. You were still going to have to earn your own things, and you weren't even looking at that. You felt superior. And that made me sad in a way I didn't know how to carry.

— I knew it was only going to get worse from there. So on the next move, I picked the worst house I could find and cut everything. All at once.

---

I took a while to process it.

— Wait — I said. — You HAVE money.

— I do, Nina.

— But we went broke when I was nine. You said it was life now.

— And it was. I didn't lie about a single word. That became life. — He gave a crooked smile. — But you thought it was because we'd gone broke. We never went broke. Living like that, spending almost nothing, saving everything... I'm richer today than I was before I took your pool away.

— I can't believe you did that to me.

— I did it for your own good. You needed that lesson.

— What a crazy lesson, Dad.

— Yeah. — He shrugged. — But look at you now.

---

And he looked. For real, a long time.

— You turned into a copy of Elizabeth. Strong. Determined. The kind who's never going to bow her head to anybody. Sometimes I think I was too heavy-handed — but then I see you and I think maybe it was the right hand. You're your mother through and through, Nina. It's bizarre how alike you are.

He paused, and his voice changed.

— The only thing that worries me is that you got too independent. You don't have people by your side. Real friends, people who stay. I have mine — they're the ones who picked me up when Elizabeth died. You still haven't found yours.

— And do I need to? — I asked.

— You do — he said. — Everybody does. Even you.
`;export{e as default};
